```{python}
#| label: setup
#| include: false

import matplotlib
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

matplotlib.rcParams.update({
    "figure.facecolor": "none",
    "axes.facecolor": "none",
    "savefig.transparent": True,
    "axes.spines.top": False,
    "axes.spines.right": False,
    "font.size": 9,
    "figure.dpi": 150,
})

rng = np.random.default_rng(20260826)

REGIONS = ["eu-north", "eu-west", "us-east", "us-west", "ap-south"]
N_PER_REGION = [420, 380, 510, 240, 90]

region_mean = dict(zip(REGIONS, rng.normal(5.1, 0.35, len(REGIONS))))

latency = pd.concat(
    [
        pd.DataFrame({"region": region, "ms": rng.lognormal(region_mean[region], 0.45, n)})
        for region, n in zip(REGIONS, N_PER_REGION)
    ],
    ignore_index=True,
)
```

The sample holds 1,640 requests across five regions. The counts are
deliberately unbalanced: `ap-south` carries fewer than a fifth of the observations of
`us-east`, which is the asymmetry the three models are being asked to handle.

```{python}
#| label: fig-latency-by-region
#| fig-cap: "Latency distribution by region"

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 3.2))
ax.boxplot(
    [latency.ms[latency.region == region] for region in REGIONS],
    tick_labels=REGIONS,
    flierprops={"markersize": 2, "alpha": 0.2},
)
ax.set_yscale("log")
ax.set_ylabel("response time (ms, log scale)")
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

Plotted on a log scale the per-region distributions are close to symmetric, which is
the first evidence that a log-normal response is the right observation model rather
than a normal one on the raw milliseconds.

```{python}
#| label: fig-sample-sizes
#| fig-cap: "Observations per region"

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 2.8))
ax.bar(REGIONS, [int((latency.region == region).sum()) for region in REGIONS])
ax.set_ylabel("requests")
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

The imbalance matters because a no-pooling fit spends exactly as much prior on
`ap-south` as on `us-east` and therefore reports a much wider interval for it. Whether
that width is honest or merely wasteful is the question the model comparison settles.

```{python}
#| label: fig-region-means
#| fig-cap: "Observed mean log-latency per region"

observed_mean = (
    latency.assign(log_ms=np.log(latency.ms)).groupby("region").log_ms.mean()
)

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 2.8))
ax.plot(REGIONS, [observed_mean[region] for region in REGIONS], "o", markersize=7)
ax.set_ylabel("mean log(ms)")
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

The observed region means sit in a band roughly $0.9$ wide on the log scale. Taken as a
$\pm 2\sigma$ range that implies a group-level standard deviation near
$\sigma \approx 0.22$, which is the quantity the hierarchical model estimates rather
than assumes.
